{
  "dom_id": "step-q274925",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Given that $A$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix, $B$ is a $2\\times 5$ matrix, and $C$ is a $5\\times 3$ matrix, in which of the following expressions are the Hadamard products well-defined?\n\nI. $AB⊙C$\n\nII. $AB⊙{C}^{T}$\n\nIII. $(AB)^{T}⊙C$",
  "worked_solution": "The Hadamard product of two matrices (or vectors) is only defined if they have the same dimensions.\n\nLet's consider each expression in turn:\n\n- The dimensions of $AB$ are $3\\times 5,$ and the dimensions of $C$ are $5\\times 3.$ Hence, the Hadamard product in expression I is *not* well-defined.\n\n- The dimensions of $AB$ are $3\\times 5,$ and the dimensions of ${C}^{T}$ are $3\\times 5.$ Hence, the Hadamard product in expression II is well-defined.\n\n- The dimensions of $(AB)^{T}$ are $5\\times 3,$ and the dimensions of $C$ are $5\\times 3.$ Hence, the Hadamard product in expression III is well-defined.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\".",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
